<section xml:id="programming-languages_the-little-computer-assembly">
  <title>The Little Computer - Assembly</title>

  <p> The Little Computer supports an <xref ref="programming-languages_assembly-languages"
          text="custom">assembly
    language</xref> that can make it easier for humans to write and read programs. In addition to
    replacing numeric commands like <q>1XX</q> or <q>902</q> with abbreviated English commands like <q>
    ADD</q> or <q>OUT</q>, Little Computer assembly allows us to think at a slightly more abstract
    level. Instead of remembering specific memory addresses so we can say <em>
      <q>add the value from location 10</q>
    </em>, we can name memory locations and then say
    things like <em>
      <q>add X</q>
    </em>. This video introduces it: </p>

  <video label="gg4siPI0sdk"
         xml:id="gg4siPI0sdk"
         youtube="gg4siPI0sdk"
         width="100%"/>
  <p> You can try the assembler here: <url href="external/LittleComputer/littleComputerAssembler.html"
         visual="external/LittleComputer/littleComputerAssembler.html">Launch
    the Little Computer assembler</url>
    <em>(Control or Command-click to open in a new window)</em>
  </p>

  <p> To test out a program after assembling it, use the Little Computer: <url href="external/LittleComputer/littleComputer.html"
         visual="external/LittleComputer/littleComputer.html">Launch
    the Little Computer</url>
  </p>

  <exercise label="programming-languages_the-little-computer-assembly-ex1">
    <statement>
      <p>
        What advantages do symbolic names provide in Little Computer assembly?
        Select all that apply.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <choices randomize="yes"
             multiple-correct="yes">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            They let a programmer refer to a meaningful name instead of remembering a specific
            memory address.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </choice>

      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            They allow references to remain correct when the program's layout or size changes.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </choice>

      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            They let the processor skip the process of executing instructions.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </choice>

      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            They make memory locations store more than one value at a time.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </choice>

      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            They remove the need for instructions to be represented in machine code.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <exercise label="programming-languages_the-little-computer-assembly-ex2">
    <statement>
      <p>
        After a Little Computer assembly program is assembled, how does the computer run it?
      </p>
    </statement>

    <choices randomize="yes">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            It executes the corresponding machine instructions.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </choice>

      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            It reads the assembly words as ordinary English sentences.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </choice>

      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            It sends the program directly to a high-level language compiler.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </choice>

      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            It runs only the labels and skips the instructions.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>


  <exercise label="programming-languages_the-little-computer-assembly-ex3">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Below is assembly code for a Little Computer program.
      </p>
      <program>
    START INP
    STA X
    SUB SECRET
    BRP EXIT
    BRA START
    EXIT LDA SECRET
    OUT
    HLT
    X DAT 0
    SECRET DAT 10
      </program>
      <p>For the lines listed below, determine what they will be assembled into. Enter the
        corresponding machine code for each line. Don't forget that the addresses are zero-based, so
        the first line is at address 00, the second line is at address 01, and so on.</p>
      <p> Address 00: <fillin mode="number"
                answer="901"/>
      </p>
      <p> Address 01: <fillin mode="number"
                answer="308"/>
      </p>
      <p> Address 02: <fillin mode="number"
                answer="209"/>
      </p>
      <p> Address 03: <fillin mode="number"
                answer="805"/>
      </p>
      <p> Address 09 (last line): <fillin mode="number"
                answer="10"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
        </test>
        <test>
          <strcmp>.*</strcmp>
          <feedback>
            <p> Incorrect. Make sure your answer is a three-digit number. <c>START</c> won't be a part of
              the answer. </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
        </test>
        <test>
          <strcmp>3\d\d</strcmp>
          <feedback>
            <p> Correct instruction type. But what address does <c>X</c> refer to? Remember that
              addresses are zero-based. </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <strcmp>.*</strcmp>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Incorrect. Make sure your answer is a three-digit number.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
        </test>
        <test>
          <strcmp>.*</strcmp>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Incorrect. Make sure your answer is a three-digit number.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
        </test>
        <test>
          <strcmp>8\d\d</strcmp>
          <feedback>
            <p> Correct instruction type. But what address does <c>EXIT</c> refer to? Remember that
              addresses are zero-based. </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <strcmp>\d05</strcmp>
          <feedback>
            <p> Correct address, but what instruction type does <c>BRP</c> correspond to? </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <strcmp>.*</strcmp>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Incorrect. Make sure your answer is a three-digit number.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
        </test>
        <test>
          <strcmp>.*</strcmp>
          <feedback>
            <p> Remember that <c>DAT</c> just means to store a value in memory. What value is being
              stored? </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
  </exercise>

</section>